---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title =
  "Fidelic: your Salesforce org, running locally. Boots in 2s, breaks on command.";
const description =
  "Fidelic is a 14MB Docker container that loads your real org's schema, runs your real Apex triggers, and injects the failures you can't test anywhere else — row locks, rate limits, tenant throttling, and your own logic's edge cases.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>A Salesforce org you can boot, break, and throw away.</h1>

  <p class="sub">
    Yours, specifically: it loads your real schema and runs your real Apex
    triggers, on your local machine, in about two seconds.
  </p>

  <p class="answer">
    Fidelic is a Salesforce emulator in a 14MB Docker container. Point your
    tests at it instead of a real org and everything behaves the same:
  </p>
  <ul class="answer-list">
    <li>same REST responses</li>
    <li>same trigger side effects</li>
    <li>same validation errors</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="answer">
    Except this org can also produce the failures no sandbox will let you stage:
  </p>
  <ul class="answer-list">
    <li>row locks</li>
    <li>rate limits</li>
    <li>tenant-wide throttling</li>
    <li>the edge cases hiding in your own logic</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="answer">
    And it's honest about the boundary. On boot it reads your triggers and labels
    each one — <strong>executed</strong>, or <strong>refused, with a reason</strong> —
    so you never get a silently wrong answer:
  </p>
  {
    /* TODO: when RECORDED-EFFECTS ships (EventBus.publish capture — a C2 family still
      unbuilt as of the async/callout milestones), swap the CaseTrigger REFUSED row for
      "≈ SIMULATED  CaseTrigger  EventBus.publish → captured, not sent". Until then it
      must read REFUSED — provable-claims rule (no advertising unbuilt behavior). */
  }
  <div
    class="terminal"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Terminal: Fidelic boots, classifies each trigger as executed or refused with a reason, then returns an UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW error when failure injection is enabled."
  >
    <pre><span class="cmd">docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./mdapi:/mdapi fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi</span>
<span class="dim">fidelic v1.0  ·  booted in 1.9s  ·  image 14 MB</span>

<span class="dim">classifying triggers against your org schema …</span>
  <span class="ok">✓ EXECUTE</span>     AccountTrigger          before/after insert, update
  <span class="ok">✓ EXECUTE</span>     ContactTrigger          after insert
  <span class="bad">✗ REFUSED</span>     CaseTrigger             EventBus.publish — not yet simulated
  <span class="bad">✗ REFUSED</span>     BillingSync.trigger:88  dynamic SOQL — not in the subset

<span class="cmd">export SFEMU_FAULT_LOCK_ON_UPDATE=true</span>   <span class="dim"># break it on command</span>
<span class="cmd">curl -sX PATCH $ORG/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Account/001xx0000…</span>
<span class="bad">&#123; "errorCode": "UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW",</span>
<span class="bad">  "message": "unable to obtain exclusive access to this record" &#125;</span></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="receipts">
    <p>
      Verified 68/68 against a conformance suite written from Salesforce's own
      docs.
    </p>
    {
      /* TODO: the suite (sf-api-conformance) is private today. When it's published,
        restore a real "Run the scorecard yourself →" link to the repo/scorecard.
        Do NOT call it "public" or link "run it yourself" until it actually is. */
    }
    <p class="soon">
      Suite + scorecard go public at launch — run it yourself then.
    </p>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="pullquote">
    <p>
      The failure injection alone is worth it — row locks and API limits are
      untestable in any real org. Fidelic makes them a config flag.
    </p>
    <cite>— Engineer at a major Salesforce partner</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="waitlist" id="waitlist">
    <h2>Early access</h2>
    <p>
      We're onboarding teams a few at a time. Leave an email; we'll reach out.
    </p>
    <form action="https://formspree.io/f/mkodpozp" method="POST">
      <input
        type="email"
        name="email"
        placeholder="you@company.com"
        aria-label="Email address"
        required
      />
      <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="Fidelic early access" />
      <input type="hidden" name="_next" value="https://fidelic.dev/thanks" />
      <button class="btn" type="submit">Get early access</button>
    </form>
  </div>
</Base>
